From abb26a08483cc05af1b6bcced519299c00527940 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:01:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Phase 02 foundation: durable SQLite project memory
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Starts "Forge's Brain" / the everyday-agent phase. Before this, every
bit of Forge's state (messages, evidence) was in-memory only and
discarded when the process exited or a new task started — "no
persistent project memory" was an explicit known limitation.

memory.py: project-scoped durable memory backed by SQLite.
- get_or_create_project(workspace_path) maps a resolved workspace path
  to a stable project id across restarts.
- remember(project_id, kind, content, source, run_id) records a
  fact, assumption, decision, or task_summary. kind is validated —
  "fact" is not interchangeable with "assumption": the distinction the
  vision doc calls for (distinguish facts from assumptions) is
  enforced, not just suggested.
- supersede(entry_id, new_content, ...) replaces an entry when new
  evidence contradicts it. The old entry is kept (active=0,
  superseded_by=<new id>), never deleted or overwritten in place, so
  history(project_id) gives an actual decision/fact timeline, not just
  a current snapshot.
- recall(project_id, kind=, limit=, query=) retrieves active entries;
  search() does substring matching over content.
- format_context() renders entries labeled by kind, for injecting into
  a task's context without pretending an assumption is a fact.

forge.py integration:
- Two new tools: `remember` (kind: fact|assumption|decision + content)
  and `recall` (kind/query/limit, all optional) — the model can
  explicitly store and retrieve project memory mid-task.
- run_task() recalls the 10 most recent memory entries for the current
  project and injects them as a separate, explicitly labeled
  "PROJECT MEMORY" message before the task prompt — distinct from (and
  not a reversal of) the earlier fix that stops raw conversation from
  leaking between unrelated tasks. That fix stays intact: `messages`
  still resets to just the system prompt every call. This is a
  deliberate, bounded, labeled channel for cross-session continuity,
  which is the explicit Phase 02 goal ("we stop starting from zero
  every session").
- run_task() records a task_summary entry at the end of every run,
  best-effort (memory unavailability never fails an otherwise-
  completed task).

Found and fixed a real bug while wiring this up: memory.py's functions
originally defaulted to `db_path: Path = DB_PATH`, which binds DB_PATH's
value at function-DEFINITION time. patch.object(memory, "DB_PATH", ...)
in tests (or any runtime override) silently did nothing for callers
using the default — tests ran against a live forge_memory.db in the
repo root instead of an isolated one. Fixed by defaulting to None and
resolving `memory.DB_PATH` inside each function body. Added
test_patching_module_db_path_affects_calls_using_the_default as a
regression guard, verified to actually fail against the old pattern
before confirming the fix.

test_memory.py (12 tests): project identity per workspace, kind
validation, supersede/history correctness, recall filtering/ordering,
search, cross-restart persistence (literally reopening the connection),
and the db_path-patching regression guard above.

test_forge.py: replaced the single leak test with three — raw-message
isolation across unrelated tasks (the original fix, confirmed intact),
project memory NOT leaking across different workspaces/projects, and
project memory correctly carrying forward within the SAME project
(the new, intended behavior). Added memory.DB_PATH patches to every
existing run_task()-driving test so none of them touch the real db.

eval_harness.py: isolates memory.DB_PATH per case, same reasoning as
the existing run_record.RUNS_DIR isolation.

60/60 tests pass (46 prior + 14 new, across test_memory.py and
additions to test_forge.py).

